+1. ArtifactData action "get", collection "state", doc_id "backupStatus". Read lastBackup, integrityCheck, restoreTest, docs, sizeBytes, weeksKept, path and syncedAt. A missing document means no backup has ever been recorded — say exactly that. The cloud writer leaves a different shape: lastBackup, verified, weeksKept, docCount {commandDeck, isaPortal}, liveDocCount, status, note and consecutiveFailures. Read those too. Treat verified true as the integrity check passing and docCount.commandDeck plus docCount.isaPortal as docs. A field the cloud writer does not record (integrityCheck, restoreTest, sizeBytes, path) is "not recorded", never a failure on its own; a restore test that has never passed is still a finding, and while weeksKept is under 8 say how many weeks are building up.
+2. Judge against the standard: a verified backup dated inside the last 8 days, integrityCheck "pass", a restore test that has passed at least once in the last 30 days, at least 8 weekly folders retained, and a document count in the same range as the previous run (a sudden drop of more than 20 percent is a red flag worth naming). A verified backup inside 8 days with only the restore test or the 8-week depth unmet is AMBER; a backup older than 8 days, an unverified one, a status of RED, or a missing document is RED.
+3. Count the live store yourself for comparison, without reading it into your conversation (it is about 1.3 MB): ArtifactData action "list", collection "state", query limit 1000, with out_dir set to a new folder named wd-count in your working directory. Count the files it lists, then delete the folder. Report how many documents exist right now versus how many the last backup captured (docs, or the sum of docCount). Never print a document's contents. If out_dir is refused, say the live count is unavailable rather than guessing.
